DESC = "Male voice in his 30s with an American accent, a warm natural documentary storyteller, conversational and sincere, clear mid pitch, medium pacing with natural pauses, engaged and curious, not acting."
PARAS = [
    # COLD OPEN
    ("McDonald's will give you free fries for one dollar. Every Friday. All you have to do is download the app.", 0.35, 501),
    ("Sounds like the best deal on the menu. But a company this big doesn't give food away for fun. Those fries are bait. And what McDonald's gets back is worth a lot more than a dollar.", 0.4, 502),
    ("Here are the tricks fast-food apps use on you, and what they cost you without you noticing.", 0.5, 503),
    # TRICK 1: THE BAIT
    ("Trick number one is the bait. Download the McDonald's app, join rewards, and you get a free large fries plus fifteen hundred bonus points. Burger King pays you in crowns. Starbucks pays you in stars.", 0.3, 504),
    ("The gift isn't the point. The point is getting a little logo onto your phone's home screen, where you'll see it every time you unlock your phone.", 0.5, 505),
    # TRICK 2: FREQUENCY
    ("Trick number two is what happens after you join. McDonald's says that in the US, the average customer visited about ten times in the year before joining its app. In the year after joining, they came twenty six times.", 0.3, 506),
    ("Same person. Same town. Same burgers. Two and a half times the visits. That's why McDonald's calls active app users its single most important digital metric. It already has nearly two hundred and ten million of them, and it wants two hundred and fifty million by the end of twenty twenty seven.", 0.5, 507),
    # DECISION: national deals -> app deals
    ("And that's why the old dollar menu is fading. A national deal gives a discount to everyone, including people who would have paid full price anyway. McDonald's CEO put it bluntly. There's a lot of waste in that.", 0.3, 508),
    ("So the deals moved into the app, where the company can choose who gets a discount, and who doesn't. When Domino's kept its seven ninety nine carryout deal, it made it digital only.", 0.5, 509),
    # TRICK 3: POINTS THAT SHRINK
    ("Trick number three. Your points are a currency, and the company controls the exchange rate.", 0.3, 510),
    ("In February twenty twenty three, Starbucks changed its rewards. The cheapest reward went from fifty stars to one hundred. A free drink went from one fifty to two hundred. Another tier jumped from two hundred to three hundred. Overnight, the stars people had saved were worth less.", 0.3, 511),
    ("And it works. Starbucks says its rewards members bring in nearly sixty percent of its US company-operated revenue. One of its executives said its best customers visit more than four times a week.", 0.5, 512),
    # TRICK 4: BILLBOARD
    ("Trick number four. The app is a billboard that lives in your pocket. It knows what you order, what time you order it, and which deals actually get you to come in.", 0.3, 513),
    ("One app analyst called it the billboard ad that keeps giving. Every lunchtime push notification is that billboard doing its job.", 0.5, 514),
    # TRICK 5: PRICES THAT MOVE
    ("Trick number five is the one that makes people angry. Prices that move.", 0.3, 515),
    ("In twenty twenty four, Wendy's said it was spending twenty million dollars on digital menu boards, and talked about dynamic pricing. The internet heard surge pricing on burgers. The backlash was so loud that Wendy's said it would not implement surge pricing.", 0.3, 516),
    ("But the idea didn't go away. This August, the Federal Trade Commission proposed a policy on personalized pricing, where companies use your data to guess how much you're willing to pay. And when Consumer Reports and two other groups tested Instacart, the same basket of groceries cost about seven percent more for some shoppers than others. Instacart said its tests were random, and it ended them.", 0.5, 517),
    # TRICK 6: CONVENIENCE TAX
    ("Trick number six is the convenience tax. A LendingTree study found that getting fast food delivered costs about eighty percent more than picking up the exact same meal yourself. Fees, tips and higher menu prices on the delivery app, all stacked together.", 0.5, 518),
    # AFTERMATH / WHAT TO DO
    ("So, should you delete the app? Not really. The deals are real, and if you were going to buy fries anyway, take the free ones.", 0.3, 519),
    ("Just play it like the company does. Turn off the notifications, so the billboard stops calling you. Use your points before the rules change. Order pickup instead of delivery. And every now and then, check the price at the counter.", 0.4, 520),
    ("Because the app is great at one thing. Getting you to come back. Take the fries. Just know what you're paying with.", 0.5, 521),
    ("We break down one business trick every day. Subscribe, and tell us which app you want us to look at next.", 0.0, 522),
]
